<div class="summary">
    <p>
        Co-author on <em>Improving LLM-Based OCR Through Document Segmentation and Semantic Prompting</em>,
        accepted to ICTAI 2026. I built the benchmarking pipeline for the layout-detection stage and found that
        much of the models’ apparent disagreement was a measurement artifact. Normalizing it cut benchmarking
        error by 40%.
    </p>
</div>

<h2>The problem</h2>
<p>
    Classic OCR reads a page as a flat string and ignores structure. Once a language model is doing the
    reading, structure matters again: a model that does not know it is looking at a table will misread it.
    The pipeline we tested was two-stage. A layout model segments the page and extracts text, and that goes
    into a vision-language model as context, on the hypothesis that the VLM returns something cleaner and
    better structured than either stage alone.
</p>
<p>
    My job was the first stage: deciding which layout model to trust. The honest answer going in was that we
    could not tell, because Detectron2, MinerU, and LayoutParser were not comparable to each other.
</p>

<h2>What I found</h2>
<p>
    The models segment a page at different granularities. One returns a paragraph as a single region; another
    returns each of its lines. Score them naively against ground truth and you penalize a model for being more
    detailed rather than for being wrong. That is a measurement artifact, not a performance difference, and it
    was dominating the rankings.
</p>
<p>
    The literature had several partial answers and no complete one, so I combined them into a bounding-box
    simplification step that brings every model’s output down to a common granularity before anything is
    scored, then re-ran the whole benchmark. Benchmarking error dropped 40%, and the rankings started reflecting
    accuracy instead of segmentation style. That is what finally let us pick the front end of the pipeline on
    evidence.
</p>

<h2>What the paper says</h2>
<ul>
    <li>MinerU wins layout IoU decisively, at 0.9654 on PRImA and 0.9994 on DocLayNet, but is roughly a hundred times slower per page than Detectron2 or LayoutParser.</li>
    <li>On prompting, all four strategies land within a couple of points on average character error rate. The separation is in the outliers, where rule-based prompting is the stable choice. The averages hid the difference; the tails showed it.</li>
</ul>

<h2>My part</h2>
<p>
    Built the benchmarking pipeline and the granularity normalization, ran the layout comparisons, and
    co-authored the paper alongside other undergraduate researchers and a faculty advisor. This is the anchor
    of the research side of what I do, and the piece I would point to for the claim that I have evaluated models
    at the pipeline level rather than only through an API.
</p>

